at  they're going through  and the thing that's so interesting to  me about the relationship between you  know spider-gwen and miles and and in  our movie all of these guys is that they  finally have like a tribe that they can  hang out with and here's somebody who  like doesn't have a real buddy and is  almost afraid to have a friend okay  right you know and I mean and so the  fact that like they kind of get to find  each other is a really like beautiful  part of the movie well as far as style  you know a lot of it you know it starts  with with Phil and Chris you know and  this this whole approach to the movie  which is that we're trying to to push  every aspect of this in a way that you  haven't seen before you know and we're  being challenged every day to think of  ways to to tell this story in a way that  that you haven't seen before and then as  far as you know the character is it  really starts with the book you know  like the book the heart of the book is  is Myles and his family and their  relationship and and that's that's the  heart of the movie it's a really  ambitious movie it's probably the most  ambitious thing about it is that it  introduces a whole new version of  spider-man  I mean comics fans and you know most of  you guys you've known miles for a while  but the the world at large doesn't  really know him and doesn't really  doesn't really have experience of a  different character kind of bearing this  mantle and the fascinating thing about  it to me is that you know the same  things that applied to Peter Parker with  great power comes great responsibility  those same ideas transfer on to another  character who couldn't be more different  than Peter in so many ways and yet the  strength of the original idea of the  character still holds firm it's still  spider-man what we're really trying to  achieve too was just a  an interpretation of a comic book that  actually made it on screen and felt  cinematic but still was was beholden to  the original source material which was a  comic where did you get the inspiration  for this new approach I've literally  torn from the pages of the comics I mean  100% and and and then it was just a  matter of looking at you know the the  process that we go through to make these  movies and and see how visually you  could you could translate that that flat  medium into a three-dimensional space  and project it and and still feel you  know the scope of a large film but also  the intimacy of the page and and the  panel on the page one of the greatest  strengths of comics is that it manages  to encapsulate in one image a really  succinct powerful story point or motion  or action and in in in our process of  animation we really tried to achieve  that same feeling mile story takes place  in an alternate dimension to our own the  spider versus basically you know all of  these alternate ways of telling this  story and all of these different people  that experience it and and the thought  of this movie was what if those  different stories intersected and what  would it do how would it shake them up  what would happen if these people met  and I think that's like the power of  this character is that we told his story  so many different ways and and like she  makes says anyone could be behind that  math and we can all imagine ourselves  behind that mask I think that's the cool  thing about spider-man and it's the  interesting thing about comics culture  and the fact that there's so many of  these movies that the story keeps  getting told and each time the that's  the differences in variations in the  ways that it gets told those are really  interesting to us well it looks  absolutely amazing thank you guys so  much for sharing that with us here at  comic-con big round of applause for our  panelists  thank you guys so much so looking  forward to this  you
 

